text:
At dusk the patient windows gleam,
then fade like lanterns in a dream;
the street grows still beneath the rain.

The wind has borrowed autumn's coat;
it trails red leaves beside the moat,
then leaves them sleeping at the gate.

River, carry my name downstream,
past willow root and broken beam;
return with only stars to keep.

The sparrow writes upon the snow
a script the morning cannot know;
by noon, each fragile mark is gone.

Old clock, be gentle with the hour;
do not disturb the sleeping flower
that closes when the daylight ends.

